# HMA scanner exemptions — keep this file as small as possible.
# Format: one rule per line.
#   - Bare path patterns exempt the file/directory from all checks.
#   - Lines prefixed with `!` exempt a specific check ID globally
#     (supports trailing `*` wildcard, e.g. `!SANDBOX-*`).
#   - `<path>:<CHECK-ID> # <reason>` exempts one file or directory from one
#     check only; the reason is required. Every other check still reads it.
#   - Any of these rules can end in `expires:YYYY-MM-DD`, written after the
#     rule and before its `# <reason>`, for example
#     `<path>:<CHECK-ID> expires:2026-12-31 # <reason>`. The rule applies
#     through that UTC day; from the next day the scan lists the line under
#     `hmaignore.errors` as expired, stops applying it, and reports its
#     findings again.
# See `src/hardening/scanner.ts:loadHmaIgnore` in hackmyagent for the parser.

# .env.example is a template file by convention. The whole purpose is
# to ship placeholder values that document what env vars the project
# expects. HMA's AST-CRED-003 ("patterns consistent with hardcoded
# secrets") fires on any KEY=value line, so it scores every .env.example
# in the ecosystem as HIGH. This exemption is safe BECAUSE we have a
# stronger invariant: .env (without the .example suffix) is in
# .gitignore via .git/info/exclude and would never be committed.
.env.example

# Go test files whose test inputs match AST-CRED-003 in a full scan
# (`secure . --ci --format json`). The literals it points at are made up
# for the test and open nothing; for example, the alphabet-pattern
# passwords the seed-log test checks are never logged, sample bearer
# tokens built from the digits and a-f, the `aim_live_` prefix an API key
# is asserted to start with, and a migration file name containing "key".
# Each line exempts its file from AST-CRED-003 only; every other check,
# including the other credential checks, still reads the file. Delete a
# line once the file stops matching.
apps/backend/cmd/server/admin_seed_log_test.go:AST-CRED-003 # alphabet-pattern passwords the test asserts are never logged
apps/backend/cmd/server/bootstrap_token_routes_test.go:AST-CRED-003 # bootstrap tokens the test mints in-process and posts to its own app
apps/backend/cmd/server/metrics_wiring_test.go:AST-CRED-003 # sample bearer tokens built from digits and a-f
apps/backend/cmd/server/migrations_renumbered_row_integration_test.go:AST-CRED-003 # a migration file name containing "key"
apps/backend/internal/application/agent_service_test.go:AST-CRED-003 # the placeholder string "encrypted-private-key"
apps/backend/internal/application/api_key_service_test.go:AST-CRED-003 # the `aim_live_` prefix an API key is asserted to start with
apps/backend/internal/application/device_auth_service_session_test.go:AST-CRED-003 # a labelled test-only JWT secret set with t.Setenv, and device codes minted in-process
apps/backend/internal/application/device_auth_service_single_use_test.go:AST-CRED-003 # device codes minted in-process by the shared test helper

# A local smoke script a developer runs against a throwaway Postgres
# container (DOCKERINJ-001, "docker exec with variable interpolation").
# Its `docker exec` takes the container name and password from the
# script's own defaults or the developer's environment, each quoted as
# one argument, and no `sh -c` parses them again, so a value cannot add
# a command. The line exempts the script from DOCKERINJ-001 only.
apps/backend/deployments/smoke/rest-list-endpoints-smoke.sh:DOCKERINJ-001 # each docker exec argument is quoted and no sh -c re-parses it

# The TypeScript SDK release smoke runs the same kind of throwaway Postgres
# container. Its `docker exec` calls take the container name, database
# name and admin email from the script's own defaults or the developer's
# environment, each quoted as one argument; no `sh -c` parses them again,
# and psql receives the email as a variable that `:'email'` quotes as one
# SQL literal. The line exempts the script from DOCKERINJ-001 only.
sdk/typescript/scripts/release-smoke.sh:DOCKERINJ-001 # each docker exec argument is quoted, no sh -c re-parses it, and psql quotes the email
